      "id": "681d333aafa307c5c93a31925882ed82337f1aa37c6afb4049460570a36c71f2",
      "title": "The day after the night that changed the country",
      "url": "https://mailchi.mp/poynter/as-democrats-swept-the-midterms-news-anchors-struggled-to-find-the-words-5288287",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T20:50:45.379Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://mailchi.mp/poynter/as-democrats-swept-the-midterms-news-anchors-struggled-to-find-the-words-5288287",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "A Democratic wave reshaped Washington overnight. Now, journalists and political commentators are examining the fallout and what comes next",
      "evidenceText": "Poynter Campaign URL Copy Twitter 0 tweets Subscribe Past Issues RSS Translate English العربية Afrikaans беларуская мова български català 中文（简体） 中文（繁體） Hrvatski Česky Dansk eesti keel Nederlands Suomi Fran&ccedil;ais Deutsch &Epsilon;&lambda;&lambda;&eta;&nu;&iota;&kappa;ή हिन्दी Magyar Gaeilge Indonesia íslenska Italiano 日本語 ភាសាខ្មែរ 한국어 македонски јазик بهاس ملايو Malti Norsk Polski Portugu&ecirc;s Portugu&ecirc;s - Portugal Rom&acirc;nă Русский Espa&ntilde;ol Kiswahili Svenska עברית Lietuvių latviešu slovenčina slovenščina српски தமிழ் ภาษาไทย Türkçe Filipino украї́нська Tiếng Việt A Democratic wave reshaped Washington overnight. Now, journalists and political commentators are examining the fallout and what comes next Email not displaying correctly? View it in your browser . OPINION The day after the night that changed the country Attendees watch early election returns at an election night event before Democratic Senate candidate Texas state Rep. James Talarico arrives to speak in Austin, Texas on Tuesday. (AP Photo/Eric Gay) When the United States woke up on Wednesday morning, it was the beginning of a new era. On Tuesday night, predictions of a blue wave in the 2026 midterms actually turned into a blue tsunami. Democrats exceeded even lofty expectations, easily flipping and taking control of the House of Representatives and the Senate. The party scored huge victories in traditionally conservative places such as Texas and South Carolina. Battleground states that have helped determine recent presidential elections &mdash; places such as Pennsylvania, Ohio, Michigan, Georgia, Arizona, New Mexico and Nevada &mdash; now all have Democratic governors. The Democrats won key Senate races in places like Maine, North Carolina, Iowa and Kansas. The Washington Post wrote , &ldquo;It was a Republican wipeout.&rdquo; A day after that historic night, here are some media thoughts looking back at Tuesday and ahead to what comes next. A repudiation The New York Times&rsquo; chief White House correspondent Peter Baker said, &ldquo;We haven&rsquo;t seen a sweep like this in 50 years.&rdquo; He called it a &ldquo;repudiation by many measures.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s the same word that was used in the one-word headline of Jonathan Lemire&rsquo;s piece in The Atlantic . Lemire wrote, &ldquo;He asked voters to make these midterm elections a referendum on him, to imagine that his name was on the ballot. They listened &mdash; and then rejected him. It is a stunning, widespread rebuke.&rdquo; In his essay for The Atlantic , Eugene Robinson wrote, &ldquo;Well, that was weird. The Donald Trump era, I mean. It&rsquo;s over now. It ended yesterday when voters handed control of both houses of Congress to the Democratic Party, along with a clear set of instructions: Stop the madness. Be honest. Be competent. Be normal. Be nice.&rdquo; Who or what is to blame? President Donald Trump talks to reporters during a tour of White House Ballroom construction on Wednesday. (AP Photo/Alex Brandon) Republicans spent Wednesday trying to figure out exactly what went wrong in this election. Sure, some tried to paint it as not totally unexpected, since the party that controls the White House almost always has trouble in the midterms. But Tuesday was a blowout, leaving the right looking for deeper answers than the typical midterm hardships. Vice President JD Vance blamed the war in Iran, writing on X , &ldquo;The reason we lost is the war.&rdquo; The New York Times&rsquo; Jonathan Swan and Maggie Haberman reported back in April that Vance was &ldquo;the figure inside the White House most opposed to a full-scale war.&rdquo; In his lengthy social media post, Vance also wrote, &ldquo;If you'd like to argue it was worth it, that losing the Senate for the next 6 years was worth it, then do so. Argue it was a worthwhile cost to pay. Agree or disagree, that's at least honest. But everyone knows why we lost. And any honest conversation about where we go from here must start there.&rdquo; Trump also conceded the war was the problem, telling reporters on Wednesday, &ldquo;It&rsquo;s largely true because it drove the gasoline (prices) up a little bit.&rdquo; Not that he regrets the war. He also said, &ldquo;At the same time, if I had to do it again, I&rsquo;d have to do it again. You can&rsquo;t let Iran have a nuclear weapon.&rdquo; After that, Trump seemed to bury his head in the sand, instead talking to reporters and aides about his new helipad installed on the White House lawn. The meltdown While the White House and other politicians in the Republican Party had their theories for Tuesday&rsquo;s dumpster fire, conservative media seemed in a full-fledged meltdown. Fox News&rsquo; Laura Ingraham tweeted , &ldquo;So was Iran still worth losing everything over? And if you really care about Israel then you have to admit how bad last night was for its future, too. Did Netanyahu not game this out?&rdquo; Podcaster and former Fox News anchor Megyn Kelly said on her show, &ldquo;This morning the Republican Party is in ruins, and maybe it needed to be.&rdquo; Then she added, &ldquo;The choice now for Republicans is to reflect, rebuild, and reinvent in the image of no man. They&rsquo;re going to have to do it on their own. They&rsquo;re going to have to learn how to win without daddy on the ballot, and to find their ethical center, and not just be puppets of Trump, not to have their entire political world revolve around Donald Trump.&rdquo; Conservative John Solomon wrote on his &ldquo;Just the News&rdquo; site , &ldquo;Exit polls showed voters were fed up with high gas prices and inflation overall, and angry that Trump put America into a prolonged conflict with Iran before fixing the inflation he inherited from Joe Biden&rsquo;s administration.&rdquo; Far-right political commentator and podcast host Matt Walsh wrote on X , &ldquo;Trump launched an unpopular and pointless war. Gas prices are too high. People can&rsquo;t afford groceries. Trump abandoned the American First message. This is why Republicans are getting crushed. It&rsquo;s very simple.&rdquo; That was just one of many Walsh tweets blasting the Republican Party, including its leadership. In another post , he wrote, &ldquo;There&rsquo;s no way to soft pedal this. It&rsquo;s a political catastrophe of historic proportions. The Republican Party needs all new leadership. None should be spared from blame, starting at the top.&rdquo; Meanwhile, far-right political activist and conspiracy theorist Laura Loomer, according to The Daily Beast&rsquo;s Laura Esposito , &ldquo;unleashed a 97-post meltdown after Democrats made sweeping gains in the House and the Senate in what was seen as a referendum on Trump.&rdquo; Esposito added, &ldquo;The Florida conspiracist went wild with baseless claims, from raging at TikTok to making racist accusations against &lsquo;terror-tied Muslims.&rsquo;&rdquo; At one point, Loomer said, &ldquo;I hate to say it, but America is over.&rdquo; Blame the media Former White House press secretary Karoline Leavitt, now a Fox News contributor, found someone to blame for the Republicans&rsquo; disastrous night on Tuesday: the media. Actually, as she calls it, &ldquo;the lying media.&rdquo; During her appearance on Fox News&rsquo;s election coverage on Tuesday night, Leavitt tried to explain what happened to the Republicans. She defended her old boss and said, &ldquo;Unfortunately, I think the American people forgot about the good policy and maybe listened to the lying media on the other side that is definitely in the backseat of the Democratic Party. And the Democrats don&rsquo;t have policy solutions.&rdquo; But Fox News&rsquo; liberal voice, Jessica Tarlov, explained to Leavitt how Americans are feeling: &ldquo;They know how much money they have.&rdquo; In a tone-deaf response, Leavitt said, &ldquo;They got more money in their pockets because of the president&rsquo;s tax cuts.&rdquo; The exchange made it abundantly clear that Leavitt is out of touch with how many Americans are feeling, which is a big reason why voters rejected Trump on Tuesday. NBC&rsquo;s good night A shot of NBC News&rsquo; election night coverage. (Photo: courtesy of NBC News) We&rsquo;ll get more updated numbers later in the week, but the overnight numbers from Nielsen&rsquo;s fast nationals showed that NBC had a big election night. The network averaged 6.8 million viewers to lead all networks. ABC was next with 5.1 million viewers. The three cable networks were within shouting distance of one another in prime time: MS NOW had 4.189 million, CNN had 4.177 million, and Fox News had 4.08 million. The struggles at CBS continued, with only 2.8 million viewers. As I said, all these are overnight numbers, which could tick up slightly when the final official figures come in. Meanwhile, NBC&rsquo;s Steve Kornacki with his &ldquo;Kornacki Cam&rdquo; had the No. 1 election livestream on YouTube last night, with a record 29 million views across all platforms. In case you missed it, I reported on election night TV coverage in my Wednesday newsletter , which included my colleague, Ren LaForme, writing about the &ldquo;Kornacki Cam.&rdquo; Another rough day Not only did the Republican Party have a rough day, but so did many conservatives who scoffed at the idea of a blue wave. The most notable was radio and podcast host Clay Travis. On Sunday, Travis tweeted , &ldquo;I nailed all my election predictions in 2024, not so much in 2022. Here are my 2026 calls with a reminder that all of you should get out and vote. Byron Donalds wins Florida Gov comfortably. Senate: Ken Paxton wins Texas, Mike Rogers wins Michigan, Jon Husted wins Ohio, Ashley Hinson wins Iowa, Susan Collins wins Maine, the real Dan Sullivan wins Alaska, Kansas, South Carolina & Nebraska aren&rsquo;t close, Republicans win senate races easily in those states. Polls are badly biased against Republicans for the 16th out of the past 17 election cycles.&rdquo; He doubled down on Tuesday, saying early in the day that no blue wave was materializing and that &ldquo;the polls are about to be proven wrong yet again.&rdquo; His predictions were massively wrong as many of his picks (Paxton, Rogers, Husted, Hinson, and Collins, for example) were crushed. But, hey, give Travis credit for admitting he was wrong. On Wednesday, he tweeted , &ldquo;I am a Moron, Republicans Get Smoked.&rdquo; He later added , &ldquo;Republicans got their asses kicked in the midterms. I was wrong about how these elections would play out, and the results were far worse than I anticipated.&rdquo; The View on the election Texas Democrat James Talarico, who won perhaps the highest-profile Senate race in the country, appeared on ABC&rsquo;s &ldquo;The View&rdquo; on Wednesday. Co-host Joy Behar talked about the &ldquo;mean&rdquo; attacks on Talarico during the campaign and asked if Talarico wanted to work with Republicans who may have been behind such attacks. &ldquo;I do,&rdquo; Talarico said. &ldquo;The time for campaigning is over, and the time to lead, the time to serve, is now starting.&rdquo; Talarico added, &ldquo;This is my favorite part of this process. This is when we actually get to solve problems. This is when we actually get to make people&rsquo;s lives easier and better and less stressful. And so I&rsquo;m looking forward to working with Republicans &mdash; including Republicans who said some mean things about me in this race &mdash; because that&rsquo;s my job. My job is to serve. My job is to make this state as good as its promise. And that means I&rsquo;ll work with anybody and everybody to make that a reality.&rdquo; Democrat Abdul El-Sayed, who won a hotly contested Senate race in Michigan, is scheduled to be on today&rsquo;s &ldquo;The View,&rdquo; while Republican Sarah Huckabee Sanders, who won a second term as Arkansas governor, is scheduled to be on the show next Monday. A Post pat on the back Washington Post executive editor Matt Murray sent a note to his staff on Wednesday morning, applauding them for their election coverage, saying the paper &ldquo;distinguished itself with the breadth, depth, fairness and authority of our coverage.&rdquo; Murray also acknowledged the turbulence the Post has undergone over the past couple of years, writing, &ldquo;All year, all of you have persevered and shown fortitude. Last night you definitively showed how the talent, teamwork and dedication of The Post continue to thrive in the service of our readers, as we simultaneously build on the best of our history and focus on our future.&rdquo; He then set the challenge moving forward, writing, &ldquo;As for the election, this morning we are only at midfield. Today and in coming days there is a tremendous amount of reporting to do on the fallout, the shifts in the country, the policy changes, and the sheer drama that we saw reflected in the vote. The story is going to be intense, exciting, and fun. Ahead of us lie two years of politics that will be as dramatic and significant for the country as any in living memory. It will require understanding, accountability, and the drive to keep finding things out and telling stories that no one else does, that compel people to come to The Post.&rdquo; Line of the night ABC late-night host Jimmy Kimmel, who has been heavily criticized by Trump over the years, poked Trump on Tuesday during his monologue, saying, &ldquo;It seems to be after all the talk about Nicki Minaj&rsquo;s ass, Jon Husted&rsquo;s ass, the only ass on Trump&rsquo;s mind tonight is the one that just got handed to him.&rdquo; Here are some more notable stories following Tuesday&rsquo;s midterms The Wall Street Journal&rsquo;s Josh Dawsey, Alex Leary and Lindsay Wise with &ldquo;How Trump Lost the Midterms.&rdquo; The Washington Post&rsquo;s Alexei Koseff, Anna Liss-Roy, Mariana Alfaro and Dan Merica with &ldquo;Democrats face a choice on Trump come January: Confrontation or compromise.&rdquo; Also in the Post, George F. Will with &ldquo;Voters rightly punished Trumpism. But please hold the giddiness.&rdquo; The Atlantic&rsquo;s Charlie Warzel with &ldquo;$500 Million Bought Elon Nothing.&rdquo; The Los Angeles Times&rsquo; Justine McDaniel and Michael Wilner with &ldquo;Democrats&rsquo; historic rout was fueled by massive voter swing to the left.&rdquo; Politico&rsquo;s John F. Harris with &ldquo;What Trump Forgot Before the Wave Crashed Down.&rdquo; The New York Times&rsquo; Michelle Cottle with &ldquo;Don&rsquo;t Be Fooled. This Is What Trump Wanted.&rdquo; Also in the Times, Carl Hulse with &ldquo;With Democrats&rsquo; Sweep, Congress Is Set to Reassert Power to Check Trump.&rdquo; Vox&rsquo;s Andrew Prokop with &ldquo;Whose fault is the GOP&rsquo;s cataclysmic defeat? 3 theories are gaining steam on the right.&rdquo; In other media news &hellip;  New York Times grows subscribers as it eyes goal of 15 million by the end of 2027 For this item, I turn it over to my Poynter colleague, Angela Fu. The New York Times ended its last quarter in the black, growing its subscriber base by 420,000 even as digital advertising revenue growth slowed, the company announced Wednesday. The Times now has 13.76 million subscribers. It reported an adjusted operating profit of $149.6 million, an increase of nearly 14% compared to the same period last year. Total revenue also grew to $768.5 million, up nearly 10% year-over-year. Chief Financial Officer Will Bardeen credited that growth to increases in digital subscription revenue and digital advertising revenue, though he noted that the latter had come in slightly below the company&rsquo;s expectations. Digital advertising revenue grew 12%, just below the &ldquo;mid-to-high-teens&rdquo; increase the company had forecast. Questioned repeatedly about this underperformance by investors on the call, Times president and CEO Meredith Kopit Levien explained that it is difficult to predict where advertisements land in a quarter. The Times, Kopit Levien stressed, has distinguished itself by focusing on its products &mdash; including its journalism &mdash; and ensuring that subscribers continue to find them valuable. &ldquo;Our economic model here at the Times is really driven a lot by subscriber engagement. That is true for both subs and ads,&rdquo; Kopit Levien said. &ldquo;So you hear us talk about consistently strong subscriber engagement, and that plays a role in the ad model too. And I continue to be really optimistic about ads as a long-term growth driver for the Times.&rdquo; Media tidbits NFL reporter Dianna Russini, who parted ways with The Athletic after questions about a possible inappropriate relationship with New England Patriots coach Mike Vrabel, could be returning to the media. ProFootballTalk&rsquo;s Mike Florio wrote , &ldquo;Per a source with knowledge of the situation, Russini has begun getting the word out to people in the league that she&rsquo;ll be returning to work &lsquo;next week.&rsquo; It&rsquo;s unclear where she&rsquo;ll be working, or what she&rsquo;ll be doing.&rdquo; The Athletic&rsquo;s Andrew Marchand did a Q&A with readers, including talking about the tumultuous Joe Buck-Troy Aikman relationship. Variety&rsquo;s Ethan Shanfeld with &ldquo;Variety&rsquo;s 10 Comics to Watch for 2026.&rdquo; More resources for journalists Protect your newsroom&rsquo;s digital journalism with Today&rsquo;s News for Tomorrow , a free program offering expert training, professional archiving tools and ongoing support to preserve local news for future generations . Apply by Dec. 11. Build a strong foundation for managing people in our five-day Essential Skills for New Managers , March 1&ndash;5, 2027, in St. Petersburg, Florida. Designed for managers with up to three years of experience, the program covers delegation, feedback, difficult conversations, managing up and leading former peers. Apply by Dec. 7. Tackle your organization&rsquo;s biggest challenges with Poynter Consulting & Coaching . From leadership development and strategic planning to AI, ethics and newsroom transformation, our experts offer customized solutions to help your team navigate change and build for the future.  Have feedback or a tip? Email Poynter senior media writer Tom Jones at tjones@poynter.org . The Poynter Report is your daily dive into the world of media, packed with the latest news and insights. Get it delivered to your inbox Monday through Friday by signing up here . And don&rsquo;t forget to tune into our biweekly podcast for even more. Support the journalism that keeps you informed. GIVE NOW ADVERTISE // DONATE // LEARN // JOBS Did someone forward you this email? Sign up here. © All rights reserved Poynter Institute 2026 801 Third Street South, St. Petersburg, FL 33701 If you don't want to receive email updates from Poynter, we understand. You can change your subscription preferences or unsubscribe from all Poynter emails .",
