      "id": "6a62a020f5da0c9d7419de3a21fe82f94d0398813bf088cd221f960c18d5553d",
      "title": "Laurels and Darts",
      "url": "https://www.firstpost.com/world/we-kept-silent-after-vucics-election-setback-serbian-broadcaster-apologises-for-years-of-media-bias-14056153.html",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T20:50:45.379Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://www.firstpost.com/world/we-kept-silent-after-vucics-election-setback-serbian-broadcaster-apologises-for-years-of-media-bias-14056153.html",
      "contentType": "text/html",
      "pageTitle": "‘We kept silent’: After Vucic’s election setback, Serbian broadcaster apologises for years of media bias",
      "pageDescription": "Serbia’s state-backed RTV has admitted to years of one-sided reporting, ignoring critical voices and failing to hold those in power accountable",
      "evidenceText": "‘We kept silent’: After Vucic’s election setback, Serbian broadcaster apologises for years of media bias LIVE TV Trending US midterms US-Iran war Europe supermarkets hacked Florida plane crash “Hills on the Edge” series LIVE TV First AI Home Live TV First AI Sections World Explainers Opinion Flaunt India Business Tech Lifestyle Health Auto Shows Vantage Firstpost America Firstpost Africa First Sports Spotlight The Spend Planet Pulse Live TV Events US Midterm Elections Brics Summit 2026 ‘We kept silent’: After Vucic’s election setback, Serbian broadcaster apologises for years of media bias Serbia’s state-backed RTV has admitted to years of one-sided reporting, ignoring critical voices and failing to hold those in power accountable Advertisement Supporters of Serbia's former President and the ruling Serbian Progressive Party (SNS) candidate for Prime Minister Aleksandar Vucic gather ahead of his pre-election rally at Liberty Square in Novi Sad, Serbia, October 16, 2026. File Image/Reuters FP News Desk | Oct 29, 2026, 05:44:13 IST See more of our coverage in your search results. Add Firstpost on Google Serbia's state-backed regional broadcaster Radio Television of Vojvodina (RTV) has made an unusual public admission of its journalistic failures, opening its flagship news bulletin with an apology for years of one-sided reporting, ignoring critical voices and failing to question those in power. The apology came amid a major political upheaval in Serbia, where an anti-corruption, student-led coalition known as the Student List reportedly defeated an alliance aligned with long-serving President Aleksandar Vucic . The broadcaster's on-air statement acknowledged that it had failed to meet its responsibility as a public-service news organisation. News anchor Goran Eror addressed viewers directly, admitting that the network had repeatedly failed to report events fairly and hold the government accountable. Advertisement Advertisement \"For years, we failed to fulfil our duty,\" Eror said during the broadcast. \"We kept silent, turned a blind eye, reported events one-sidedly and failed to ask the necessary questions. For that, we owe our viewers an apology.\" Following the statement, RTV aired footage that had previously been left out of its coverage, showing thousands of people celebrating the Student List's victory in cities across Serbia. Shortly afterwards, the broadcaster's acting editor-in-chief, Nenad Cacic, submitted his resignation. More from World Railway station disaster fuelled anti-corruption protests The broadcaster's admission follows nearly two years of anti-corruption protests that have challenged Serbia's political establishment. Public anger intensified after the collapse of a railway station canopy in Novi Sad in November 2024, which killed 16 people. The disaster triggered allegations of government negligence, corruption in public infrastructure projects and political favouritism under Vucic's administration. Students emerged as a key force in the protests, demanding accountability and action against corruption. State-owned and pro-government media outlets faced growing criticism during the protests for overlooking opposition voices and portraying demonstrators negatively. According to the independent election monitoring organisation Crta, government officials received more than 75 per cent of political coverage on national broadcasters during the election campaign. The Student List, meanwhile, was portrayed negatively in more than 80 per cent of its appearances in news segments, according to the figures cited in the report. Advertisement Advertisement Serbia was ranked 104th out of 180 countries in the 2026 World Press Freedom Index, its lowest position on record. Pro-government broadcasters face public anger The political shift also brought scrutiny of private broadcasters and tabloids widely criticised for their pro-government coverage. In Belgrade, protesters entered the newsroom of Informer TV , interrupting its live broadcasts and demanding the arrest of its owner, Dragan Vucicevic. Crowds also gathered outside Pink TV , another broadcaster associated with pro-government coverage. The Independent Association of Journalists of Serbia said it would formally seek the revocation of Informer TV's broadcasting licence, citing what it described as repeated violations of legal requirements. Vucic condemned the confrontations at newsrooms, describing them as an attack on freedom of speech. Handpicked stories, in your inbox Global stories. Indian perspective. Zero noise. Subscribe Now No Spam. Unsubscribe Any Time. Add Firstpost on Google Follow Firstpost on Google . Get insightful explainers , sharp opinions , and in-depth latest news on everything from geopolitics and diplomacy to World News . Stay informed with the latest perspectives only on Firstpost. Tags Protests First Published: Oct 29, 2026, 05:44:13 IST Home World we kept silent after vucics election setback serbian broadcaster apologises for years of media bias Advertisement Advertisement Up Next Home Videos First AI Quick Reads Live TV",
