      "id": "6b8c0542f1e403401f829e76488e38450e23835645783e2c8710c14f9d8126b2",
      "title": "The day after the night that changed the country",
      "url": "https://justthenews.com/politics-policy/elections/democrat-bloodbath-reminder-elections-still-are-about-economy-first-and",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T20:50:45.379Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://justthenews.com/politics-policy/elections/democrat-bloodbath-reminder-elections-still-are-about-economy-first-and",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "Republican bloodbath a reminder elections still are about the economy, first and foremost | Just The News",
      "pageDescription": "“I don&#039;t think we should over analyze it,&quot; conservative pollster Rich Baris says. &quot;Republicans were the party that promised to fix the economy and inflation, put America first, ahead of the war lobby and the Washington grifters, and we didn&#039;t do it. And they punished us for it. And we deserved it.&quot;",
      "evidenceText": "Republican bloodbath a reminder elections still are about the economy, first and foremost | Just The News Skip to content Republican bloodbath a reminder elections still are about the economy, first and foremost “I don't think we should over analyze it,\" conservative pollster Rich Baris says. \"Republicans were the party that promised to fix the economy and inflation, put America first, ahead of the war lobby and the Washington grifters, and we didn't do it. And they punished us for it. And we deserved it.\" By John Solomon Published: November 4, 2026 1:01am Article Dig Deeper Democrats swept elections coast to coast on Tuesday night and seized control of Congress and prized statehouses in the deep red South, as Americans punished Republicans for failing to finish the job of taming inflation and fixing an economy about to be transformed by artificial intelligence. Hispanic, young and independent voters who powered Donald Trump’s improbable comeback in 2024 markedly shifted their allegiance to Democrats in the midterms, even putting a Democrat in a U.S. Senate seat in Kansas for the first time in a century and a Hispanic newcomer into the Texas gubernatorial mansion. Exit polls showed voters were fed up with high gas prices and inflation overall, and angry that Trump put America into a prolonged conflict with Iran before fixing the inflation he inherited from Joe Biden’s administration. Three quarters of voters in Tuesday's election described the U.S. economy as in \"rough shape,\" and 39% said it was the primary reason for how they voted. Forty-four percent said their finances were worse today than when Trump took office. In many ways, Tuesday night resembled -- in reverse -- the 1994 election that ousted Democrats from power coast to coast during the Gingrich revolution and set the GOP on its modern path to power over similar frustration about affordability. “I don't think we should over-analyze it,\" conservative pollster Rich Baris, who first sounded warnings of a GOP election bloodbath this summer, told Just the News early Wednesday. \"Republicans were the party that promised to fix the economy and inflation, put America first, ahead of the war lobby and the Washington grifters, and we didn't do it. And they punished us for it. And we deserved it.\" Scott Rasmussen, a veteran GOP pollster who created a scoreboard that predicted Tuesday's outcomes accurately, agreed that Americans fired Republicans for failing to get the job done more than placing their trust in the left. \"Last night was an expression of economic frustration. It's the same message they sent two years ago when they sent Trump back to the White House,\" Rasmussen told Just the News. \"Voters had hoped Trump would make the economy work for all Americans. Instead, the war in Iran pushed up gas prices and left voters just as frustrated as they were under Biden.\" The losses for Republicans were staggering: three decades of statewide GOP rule in Texas were swept away, long-held Republican Senate seats in Iowa, Kansas and Ohio were lost alongside the governor’s mansions and enough seats in the House were seized nationwide to give Democrats a substantial working majority. The losses thrust the last two years of Trump’s second presidency into hostile territory, a painful reality for a president who held 29 rallies, 40-plus telephone rallies, spent $450 million of his own PAC money in a relentless bid to keep Republicans in control in the final month of the election. Trump promised to accept the results. “I think we’ll work with the Democrats, and we’ll get along very well with them,\" he told The Wall Street Journal . \"If we don’t, we’ll be very tough on them, they’ll be very tough on us.\" He said the Iran war was \"a piece of\" the election dynamic, particularly its impact on gas prices, but that he had no regrets on his decision to bomb Iran. “I don’t think about it. I did the right thing. I would have done it again,” he said. The night produced numerous firsts: black Democrat governors in Georgia and Wisconsin, a Hispanic female governor in the Lone Star State, numerous socialists in Congress and state Legislatures and a U.S. senator-elect who ran as a pastor believing God was nonbinary and humans have six genders. And while many liberal pundits declared the election was an affirmation of a far-left lurch in America, exit polls showed anger and distrust for both parties and a clear rejection of socialism becoming mainstreamed in American politics. Fifty-seven percent of voters favored capitalism compared to just 33% who said they held a favorable view of socialism. \"I talked with lots of voters these last few weeks. It's very clear that they did not embrace the left, they just repudiated us,\" Baris said. \"I know just from speaking to voters they didn't want to do it to us, but they felt they had to do it. And they did.\" Tuesday's results will do more than just realign the balance of power in Washington. It will realign the forces that shape the 2028 election. Trump's top lieutenants and most likely successors, Vice President J.D. Vance and Secretary of State Marco Rubio, won't easily be able to escape Tuesday's verdict, opening the door for possible contenders to test the waters from outside the Trump orbit. And that race begins in earnest on Wednesday in both parties. Related Articles Elections GOP lawmaker defeats fellow Republican incumbent after redistricting pits them against each other By Kevin Killough Elections California voters reject billionaire tax measure By Misty Severi Elections Arizona GOP Rep. Eli Crane wins reelection in close midterm race By Misty Severi Unlock unlimited access No Ads Within Stories No Autoplay Videos VIP access to exclusive Just the News newsmaker events hosted by John Solomon and his team. Support the investigative reporting and honest news presentation you've come to enjoy from Just the News. Learn more Top Stories Congress House Republicans choose Jim Jordan to become minority leader Politics & Policy Senate Republicans pauses leadership votes to hear demands from members about lame duck strategy Sponsored Join AMAC — The conservative voice for freedom, faith, and America’s future Religion Virginia man caught, arrested for trying to sneak loaded gun, knives into DC synagogue on Shabbat Crime House Oversight Republicans release final report on Epstein investigation, clears Trump of crimes Trending Freshly re-elected Maryland Gov. Moore accused of inflating his Afghanistan service Alaska Sen. Murkowski plans to become ranking member on powerful spending committee Days after Democrats sweep state government leadership, Houston mayor issues anti-ICE directive Trump pushes Senate to confirm his outstanding nominees before the end of the year Democrats may have accidentally triggered Republicans to abolish filibuster Just the News Spotlight Just News, No Noise Health Weight loss drugs, such as Ozempic, to carry FDA warning about rare vision-loss side effects Courts & Law Trump nominates 3 more federal judges as Senate mulls lame duck plans Energy Average US gasoline prices fall below $4 per gallon for the first time since July All Things Trump Trump pushes Senate to confirm his outstanding nominees before the end of the year Congress Senate advances bipartisan permitting reform bill in return from midterms recess Support Just the News The Latest Politics & Policy Tom Emmer battles back challenge to keep GOP whip Transportation Investigators recover cockpit recorder from doomed Learjet, may provide details on what happened Politics & Policy Ro Khanna calls for banning sports 'prediction markets'",
