      "id": "75ba69586812903ccedac6a6898401a2e9b38ead09433dd357586dd8adb8061c",
      "title": "How ordinary people build solutions",
      "url": "https://us.list-manage.com/KUKmwD5_wfx",
      "category": "uncategorized",
      "checkedAt": "2026-11-10T20:50:45.379Z",
      "httpStatus": 200,
      "finalUrl": "https://reasonstobecheerful.world/how-nyc-students-saved-school-lunch",
      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "How NYC Students Saved School Lunch",
      "pageDescription": "Teens are reshaping citywide menus, redesigning cafeteria spaces and proving youth advocacy gets real results.",
      "evidenceText": "How NYC Students Saved School Lunch How NYC Students Saved School Lunch Teens are reshaping citywide menus, redesigning cafeteria spaces and proving youth advocacy gets real results. Credit: Chalermpon Poungpeth / Shutterstock By: Tolu Olasoji October 8, 2026 This story is part of: Born Into It This story is part of Born Into It, a series about the solutions helping young people thrive in a complex world. It is supported by UPIC Health . T here were three clear reasons why Leslie Gomez Rivera avoided the cafeteria at her high school in Brooklyn: She didn’t like the food, the room felt hectic and there was a stigma attached to eating school meals. She preferred the library, where she could talk with friends, although sometimes that meant going without lunch altogether. So when Rivera heard about Youth Food Advocates (YFA), an initiative that trains New York City students to research issues around school food and advocate for changes, she realized she might be able to do something to make the whole situation better. With the help of YFA, the 16-year-old developed a survey and circulated it around Midwood High. She wanted to know what her fellow students liked to eat, what they wanted on the menu, why they stayed away from the cafeteria and what might make them come back. Participants at a Youth Food Advocates leadership training. Credit: Community Food Advocates “I had the answers to [some of the questions],” Rivera recalls, “but I wanted to know what my peers thought.” Around 100 students responded out of roughly 4,000 across Midwood’s two buildings. Their answers complicated the verdict that school lunch was simply bad. Participants singled out popcorn chicken, sauces, fruit and salads as popular items. They also raised concerns about food quality and limited choices, and some wanted more of the foods they ate at home. This was back in late 2020. By then, NYC had already made lunch free for every public school student following years of advocacy that included student organizers from YFA. But free food did not necessarily equate to food students wanted to eat, or a cafeteria where they wanted to spend their lunch period. When Rivera joined YFA as the sole student representative for Midwood, she began to realize the potential impact she could have. “I felt like this was something that not only affects my everyday [life], but that the next generation … would be affected,” Rivera says. “And if I could do something about it, I would.” She brought her survey findings to Midwood’s cafeteria manager and principal, and says the manager was surprised that a student had arrived with organized feedback but was receptive to hearing it. “At the end of the day, this is what you guys are eating,” the manager told her, according to Rivera. “I would love to know your input.” Weighed down by negative news? Enter your email below to receive our smart, uplifting newsletters. Or click here to choose exactly which ones you want During that conversation, the manager highlighted the fact that, in the wake of Covid, the number of students coming to the cafeteria had plummeted. Rivera took this on board and ultimately decided to discuss it with Midwood’s student leadership team, which began announcing that the redesigned cafeteria was open again and promoting what was on the menu. The space eventually returned to full use, something Community Food Advocates (CFA) — the organization that runs YFA — later credited Rivera for helping achieve. “This was one of the pivotal moments when I realized that if you want it done, you can do it yourself,” Rivera says. A spokesperson for New York City Public Schools, the largest public school system in the United States, says student input has become “a critical part” of improving meals in the city’s public schools. She says that — in addition to considerations such as federal nutrition requirements and cultural representation — all new menu items currently undergo student taste testing. New items must receive at least a 70 percent student approval rating before consideration for a spot on the citywide menu. YFA students have also helped develop a toolkit for schools rolling out redesigned cafeterias, with recommendations including the introduction of deli-style stations that display food more prominently, offer students more choice and cut queue times by replacing the more traditional single line system. Another focus has been on newer, more comfortable seating intended to make the room feel more welcoming and social. By December 2025, more than 200 cafeterias had been renovated. CFA says lunch participation has risen 35 percent at redesigned high schools, based on its analysis of city data. Pamela Koch, faculty director of the Laurie M. Tisch Center for Food, Education and Policy at Teachers College, Columbia University, says students are “best positioned” to give feedback because “they are the ones that are eating the food every day.” But, she adds, to be useful it has to be specific. “When you just say, ‘Oh, the food isn’t good,’ nobody knows what to do about it,” she says. Rivera says YFA taught her that precision. She credits then-program leader Anna Lilia Araiza with educating her about school food history, research and how to frame questions that could produce useful answers. “They provided me not only with the information [and] the tools, but they empowered me to advocate for myself,” Rivera says. That information also has to reach adults who can act on it. Wendi Gosliner, a researcher at the University of California’s Nutrition Policy Institute, describes student engagement as “a bridge with food service.” She says cafeteria staff operate within budgets, federal rules and other constraints, so the goal is “to not vilify food services, but rather to collaborate with them.” Part of that work, Koch says, is learning whether a problem can be addressed by cafeteria staff, city officials or at the federal level. This kind of student advocacy work can also produce something less visible than a revised menu. Koch, who worked on the Lunch 4 Learning campaign to bring universal free school lunch to all public school students in NYC, has watched young advocates testify at City Hall and describes it as “incredibly moving.” “They are having a voice in creating the policies that will impact not only their lives, but also the lives of all of the other students now and into the future,” she says. Through YFA, young people work together to develop the leadership, research and communication skills required to advocate for systemic change. Credit: Community Food Advocates Iyeshima Harris-Ouedraogo remembers how empowering such an experience can be. As a teenager involved in Lunch 4 Learning, she testified at City Hall. After one hearing, her group met then-Mayor Bill de Blasio, who told the students he supported universal meals. “I thought that was amazing,” she says. The encounter made government feel more accessible to her. Citywide universal free school meals followed in 2017. Years later, Harris-Ouedraogo says the relationships she began building through student advocacy still shape her work. Now executive director of Green Guerillas, a community garden advocacy organization, she sometimes engages with public officials she first met through organizing as a young person — a familiarity she says can carry across years and campaigns. Wait, you're not a member yet? Join the Reasons to be Cheerful community by supporting our nonprofit publication and giving what you can. Join Cancel anytime Rivera also later worked at Green Guerillas, teaching and facilitating civic engagement with community gardeners, and has testified at a city budget hearing. “Encouraging others to advocate for themselves and advocate for what they deserve is important and rewarding,” she says. “YFA opened that door for me, and I have been pursuing it ever since.” After she left Midwood, her younger siblings attended the same school and used its enhanced cafeteria. They found lunch more enjoyable. Now 22, Rivera has a simple message for high schoolers. “We usually think that nobody’s gonna listen to us. But … just because you’re a young person doesn’t mean that you automatically don’t have ideas or they’re not valid.” Scrolling photos courtesy of Community Food Advocates. Tolu Olasoji Tolu Olasoji is an Emmy and Telly Award-winning journalist, producer, documentary photographer and artist. He was a contributing editor at Reasons to be Cheerful, primarily covering Africa. His reporting examines how people navigate systems and build responses to social, political, economic and environmental challenges. His work has appeared in Al Jazeera, The Washington Post, VICE, Quartz and other publications, and has received support from the Authors League Fund, PEN America and Investigative Reporters and Editors. He lives in Jersey City, New Jersey. Related Stories Farms + Food More California Schools Than Ever Are Embracing Vegan Meals Frida Garza October 18, 2024 6 min read How have plant-based lunches spread so successfully? Credit environmentally conscious students — and a handful of state funding programs. Civic Engagement This High School Gave Spending Power to Students Asher Lehrer-Small February 23, 2023 8 min read Participatory budgeting in Rhode Island began with handing $10,000 to teens. Now this form of direct democracy is spreading across the state. Health Inside the Teen-Run Hotline Meeting America’s Mental Health Crisis Michaela Haas January 23, 2026 6 min read Teen Line’s peer support can be a lifeline for callers. The volunteers behind it say it’s transformative for them too. Weighed down by negative news? Our smart, bright newsletters deliver the uplift you’ve been looking for. Or click here to choose exactly which ones you want My bookmarks My bookmarks Listed below are all the stories you've bookmarked. To be able to bookmark stories on RTBC, you need to accept cookies ( click here to adjust your cookie settings