      "evidenceText": "HBCU Students March to the Polls Ahead of 2026 Midterms - Capital B News Close Search for: Search Search 🤍 Donate Newsletters For the Record Archive Criminal Justice Education Environmental Justice Health Money Politics & Policy Rural Issues Capital B Atlanta Capital B Gary Capital B Augusta About Capital B Our Team Careers Inside Capital B Advertise With Us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X TikTok Bluesky Close Search 🤍 Donate Newsletters For the Record Archive Criminal Justice Education Environmental Justice Health Money Politics & Policy Rural Issues Capital B Atlanta Capital B Gary Capital B Augusta About Capital B Our Team Careers Inside Capital B Advertise With Us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X TikTok Bluesky Skip to content Students at Morgan State University in Baltimore gather at Holmes Hall ahead of a march to the polls on Election Day. (Alecia Taylor/Capital B) We Write the Untold Stories of Black America Read them in Seen & Heard, our free, twice-weekly newsletter. Sign up BALTIMORE – Morgan State University students chanted, “This is what democracy looks like!” and “There’s hope if you vote!” as they marched Tuesday to the nearest polling place just a mile away from campus. As some students entered the polling place, the crowd cheered them on, encouraging students and other Baltimore residents as they exercised their right to vote. Many of the students who marched voted early, including Ugonnia Metume-Ibe. The 19-year-old political science major at the university said preparing for the ballot wasn’t easy. With the help of her sister and her friend, she studied the Maryland ballot, the more than 10 ballot questions, and the candidates for local and state offices. “After making sure my paperwork was good I sat down, and it made me feel a little bit ready,” she said. Metume-Ibe recalled the quick breath she had before voting for the first time: “When I casted my vote, it was just like, oh my gosh, I did it!” Jah’leya Hudson, 21, said she cast her first ballot as an official Maryland voter this year. Originally from California, she said she and her family have been directly impacted by federal cuts and programs meant to assist low-income families. Her Pell Grant was slashed in half and her mother, who has four other children, recently lost their Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program benefits. “There’s so many like-minded people here,” said Hudson, who studies political science at Morgan State. “We’re all here for the same reason, which is true change.” Madison Council, 21, brought Cooper, 2, to the polls with her during Morgan State University’s march to the polls on Election Day. (Alecia Taylor/Capital B) Morgan State joins the long list of historically Black colleges that have organized demonstrations leading up to Election Day. From the Mid-Atlantic to the Deep South, thousands of HBCU students took matters into their own hands with “Blackout” and “March to the Polls” protests. North Carolina A&T State, Norfolk State University , Bowie State University, Alcorn State University , and students from dozens of other HBCUs have been marching on behalf of young Black people. Despite assumptions from older generations that Gen Z isn’t civically engaged, students tell Capital B that they’ve been pushing back. They are also following in the footsteps of HBCU students from previous generations who marched to polls and statehouses.  Omolayo Adegoke, 18, joined other Morgan State University students in a march to the polls on Election Day. (Alecia Taylor/Capital B) At Jackson State University, Camrynn Wimberly and other students marched from their campus to Mississippi’s state capitol weeks before Election Day. Wimberly held a sign that said “Mississippi needs your courage,” but also “Mississippi needs our rage.” For Wimberly, a lot is at stake for Black Mississippians. In the past year, four young Black people and children have either been killed or died under suspicious circumstances.  “Our demand and what we want to come out of this is that everyone has a right to a competent and thorough investigation,” she said. “We want an investigation into the Mississippi state’s medical examiner.”  Those demands can’t be fulfilled through social media chatter, she said. It comes from electing officials that align with your demands. It was her rage over the death of Nolan Wells and the aftermath that fueled her passion during this midterm election. It’s the only way to make a true difference, she said. That’s why the 22-year-old, her classmate Jakari Holland, and other JSU students organized the march. Their goal: inspire voters and elected officials to take action at the state and federal level. Before the march in October, Wimberly and others made signs together. During the protest, she heard more disheartening conversations about the lack of accountability at the local and federal levels, attaching the trend to the state. “That’s why I don’t send my kids to be around white people!” “Oh, well, that could never happen here!” Originally from Hattiesburg, Mississippi, she took those comments from her community to heart when the details of Wells’ disappearance and death hit the news. “I know politics is hard, I know it’s confusing, overwhelming and hard to understand,” she said about voting restrictions and redistricting. “It’s hard on purpose; it’s inaccessible on purpose.” Jackson State University students marched from their campus to Mississippi’s state capitol weeks before Election Day. (Ahmari Butler) Dispelling the myth that Gen-Z is disengaged  Jayla Jones stood among other Florida A&M University students and other community members on Oct. 16 at the state Capitol in Tallahassee, Florida. The Wells investigation and similarities to the Trayvon Martin case were on their minds. In 2013, FAMU students joined Tallahassee Dreams, a civil rights group that organized a 31-day sit-in at the same building following George Zimmerman’s acquittal in the death of the Black teen. While the connection made Jones feel powerful as she delivered a speech demanding a change in the justice system, it also frustrated her that students were back in the same position so many years later. “I was like, ‘This sounds very familiar,’ because it’s still happening,” said Jones, a fourth-year political science major at FAMU. “Twelve years later, the terror on Black men, women, and children has not stopped.”  Florida A&M University students walk to the polls during an Oct. 15 event in Tallahassee, Florida. (Jeffery D. Adams/Florida A&M University via Getty Images) Jones and other students from FAMU Students for a Democratic Society, a student coalition in Tallahassee, have been protesting for the safety of Black and brown people since last year. This semester alone, Jones has attended several demonstrations against the university’s agreement to aid U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement officers if needed. In her eyes, the agreement, like the death of Wells and other young Black people, threatens the rights of young Black and brown people in this country. Jones pointed to how the crackdown on immigration within the Haitian community led to the death of Pierre Damas Bel from Ohio, who took his own life after being ordered to wear an ankle monitor.  “It’s shameful, especially on a campus where you know where we consider ourselves family and our administration is not protecting their Haitian students,” Jones said.  While protesting at Florida’s state Capitol, she said an older man passing by stopped to join. He told her that when he was young, this was how his generation got people to listen. More than 500 miles away on Oct. 15, HBCU students mobilized to exercise their right to vote. In Greensboro, North Carolina, thousands from North Carolina A&T State University and Bennett College marched for 40 minutes after officials removed their early polling location from campus.  NCA&T students, led by Protect Ours, a Gen-Z-led student group not affiliated with the university, staged several peaceful protests before the Guilford County Board of Elections voted earlier this year not to put a polling site on the college campus. Now, the group has been raising funds and partnering with community organizations to organize shuttle rides to the nearest polling place more than a mile away, according to The A&T Register , the university’s student news publication.  While some older people claim Gen Z isn’t civically engaged, Tevon Blair said this isn’t true. The Dillard University graduate and co-founder of a nonprofit that supports student leaders at HBCUs said he witnessed firsthand how older generations view younger generations as incapable of leading. As a student, he and others proved the critics wrong. “In 2020, we saw so many people trying to mobilize HBCU students during this moment, and there we were reminded of what it was like being a college student during 2016,” he said. “There were so many people telling us who to vote for rather than asking us ‘Who are you and what are the issues that we care about?’”  Four years later, the nonprofit launched the Vote HBCU program, which registered more than 800 students to vote in less than a month at 10 different HBCUs.  “We’re doing everything in our power to mobilize on these campuses, mobilize inside of our community, and be a voice for all so future generations don’t have to go through what we go through,” Holland, the Jackson State student, said on Instagram .  He mentioned the disinvestment in HBCUs and overpolicing of Black communities, pointing to the death of Kohen Wiley, a 1-year-old boy who was fatally shot by a Mississippi police officer in June.  Holland is just one of the many students selected to be a part of Blair’s program, to spotlight HBCU student leaders who are stepping up to lead the demonstration popping up across the country. In the video, he shared that his father voted in the midterm elections for the first time when Holland was just 1 years old – and it’s inspired him ever since. “The youth have always been at the heart of the movement when it comes to civil rights and Black Americans in general, the perfect age,” Wimberly said. “It’s a whole lot different than expecting mid-career professionals to take to the streets.” This story has been updated. Alecia Taylor Alecia Taylor is the national education reporter at Capital B. More by Alecia Taylor “I am so proud to be part of Capital B. It’s what journalism needs to be.”  — Joan, Capital B Member Join Joan, donate now! Donate We make it easy for you to stay informed Get the biggest stories of Black America delivered to your inbox each week for free. Sign up for our national newsletter Seen & Heard now! Sign up Stay in the loop with stories you care about With our free newsletter, Seen & Heard, you can get the biggest stories of Black America delivered to your inbox each week. Sign up We make it easy for you to stay informed Get the biggest stories of Black America delivered to your inbox each week for free. Sign up for our national newsletter Seen & Heard now! Sign up Sign up to the For The Record newsletter! Get hand-picked stories from Black history straight to your inbox Sign up Sign up for Capital B’s free twice-weekly newsletter to get the untold stories of Black America in your inbox: Sign up Link Copy link",
