      "evidenceText": "DRC Orders Probe into Illegal Mining Following Major Copper and Cobalt Seizure | OCCRP DRC Orders Probe into Illegal Mining Following Major Copper and Cobalt Seizure News November 3, 2026 The Democratic Republic of the Congo will open an investigation into suspected mining fraud and widespread illegal mining, following the seizure of thousands of tons of copper and cobalt as well as more than 500 trucks and other machines, according to a statement by the Justice Ministry. The probe comes two weeks after Congo's mines minister suspended the head of the state agency overseeing artisanal and ​small-scale mining, accusing him of failing to prevent illegal ‌mining on licensed concessions. As the world’s largest producer of cobalt and second-largest producer of copper, the Democratic Republic of the Congo has long struggled with unauthorized miners and cross-border smuggling rings encroaching on industrial concessions held by foreign operators. November 3, 2026 November 3, 2026 Support fearless reporting. Unlock crucial insights. Donate Your donation powers investigations that expose organized crime and corruption worldwide. As a supporter, you’ll also gain access to events and insights only available to our donors. Give today and get the inside take. Donate Related Articles 1 week ago Criminal Probe Launched in South Africa Over Contraband Baobabs Bound for Georgia Environment South Africa 1 year ago Congolese NGOs Pursue Action Against US Company Awarded Lake Kivu Methane Concession Environment Democratic Republic of the Congo 5 years ago Report: Criminals in Central Africa Profit from New Surge in Gold Prices Environment Central African Republic 1 week ago Criminal Probe Launched in South Africa Over Contraband Baobabs Bound for Georgia Environment South Africa 1 year ago Congolese NGOs Pursue Action Against US Company Awarded Lake Kivu Methane Concession Environment Democratic Republic of the Congo 5 years ago Report: Criminals in Central Africa