      "contentType": "text/html; charset=UTF-8",
      "pageTitle": "As Democrats swept the midterms, news anchors struggled to find the words - Poynter",
      "pageDescription": "‘Wow’ was the word of the night as a historic blue wave stunned election-night broadcasters",
      "evidenceText": "As Democrats swept the midterms, news anchors struggled to find the words - Poynter As Democrats swept the midterms, news anchors struggled to find the words - Poynter DONATE What do you want to search? Submit Sign In | Membership News Training Ethics Fact-Checking Media Literacy AI Events Future of Public Media Newmark Ethics Center IFCN MediaWise PolitiFact Donate About Us Press Freedom Watch The Poynter 50 Podcasts Artificial Intelligence Business & Work Reporting & Editing Fact-Checking Ethics & Trust Local News Journalism, Media & Communication Training Poynter Membership Categories Consulting & Coaching For ACES Members For Local News Funders All Categories Broadcast & Visual Journalism Business & Work Ethics Fact-Checking & Media Literacy Leadership Reporting & Editing Writing Poynter ACES Introductory Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Intermediate Certificate in Editing Poynter ACES Advanced Certificate in Editing Workshops and Coaching Tools for Funders Ethics Ethics Training Ethics Articles AI Ethics Public Editor Project Get Ethics Advice Fact-Checking Articles IFCN IFCN Grants International Fact-Checking Day Teen Creator Network PolitiFact MediaWise Media Literacy Resources AI Literacy Ambassadors Teen Creator Network Research & News International en Español MediaWise Training All Events The 2026 Poynter Journalism Prizes Commentary Newsletters The MS NOW set at the start of the network’s election coverage on Tuesday night. (Courtesy of MS NOW) By: Tom Jones November 4, 2026 Wow. Tuesday was a historic day in America as one of the most pivotal midterm elections played out on our televisions, computers and phones. And the media reaction can be summed up in one word: Wow. On Tuesday night, the word “wow” might have been uttered on TV more than any other interjection ever. Anchors, analysts, election experts — you name it — all ran out of words to express their surprise at a variety of races. NBC’s Steve Kornacki might have been the league leader in “wows.” Or it might have been CNN’s Jake Tapper. Or maybe it was MS NOW’s Rachel Maddow. Even Fox News anchors, stunned by Tuesday’s results, couldn’t help but blurt out the word. But who could blame them on a night full of surprises as a blue wave crashed over the country and Democrats took control of the Senate and House of Representatives? And it didn’t even take all night. It was just a little past 10 p.m. Eastern time when MS NOW’s breaking news banner read “Dems overperform in races across the country in a blue wave.” CNN’s breaking news banner: “Dems on track for midterm rout as voters reject Trump’s GOP.” The New York Times subheadline read, “Stunning upsets across the country in national rebuke of Trump.” The Washington Post’s blaring headline: “Democrats pile up big wins.” When CBS News opened the 10 p.m. Eastern hour of its coverage, co-anchor Norah O’Donnell said: “It looks like a blue wave.” At 11:35 p.m. — early by election standards — CNN’s Dana Bash said, “This is absolutely monumental.” She said that moments after it became official that the Democrats had flipped the Senate after having earlier flipped the House. Even with all the surprising results in places such as Texas and Kansas, perhaps the most stunning was the South Carolina Senate race, where Annie Andrews beat Republican Darline Graham, the sister of late Sen. Lindsey Graham. It was the first time this millennium that South Carolina elected a Democrat to the Senate. “Frankly, it’s stunning,” Tapper said. He also called it “shocking,” and you had the feeling he wanted a thesaurus to find more ways to describe it. “Stunning news,” he said. That was the theme of the night during the coverage. Words like “stunning” and “shocking” and, most of all, “wow.” Here’s a look back at some of the highlights of Tuesday night’s coverage of the midterms, with plenty more to come in the days ahead. Outstanding coverage Let’s start here: If you’re a political junkie, Tuesday night was like the Super Bowl of news. Every major broadcast and cable news network responded with coverage that was informative, entertaining and fascinating, especially because of the big-map technology that gets better and more insightful with every election. The only downside? You can’t watch all the stations at once. I found myself mostly gravitating toward CNN and MS NOW, and I particularly looked forward to big board coverage provided by CNN’s John King. It’s on nights like this when King and NBC’s Steve Kornacki stand out. (More on Kornacki in a moment.) But all the networks had their moments — just too many to completely chronicle here. In the end, election coverage in this country has never been more interesting. You can describe the coverage with one word: wow. The thrill of the Kornacki Cam For this item, I turn it over to Poynter’s managing editor Ren LaForme. Steve Kornacki, the NBC News data analyst known for his khakis and big board, is inescapable on the network most election nights. But NBC brought back the ultimate fix for his diehards Tuesday night: an all-Kornacki livestream . The network experimented with the format during this year’s special elections and primaries, but this was my first time tuning in. Starting at 5:30 p.m. Eastern, Kornacki did double duty — navigating county-by-county numbers on his giant map for the stream while occasionally switching over to NBC’s live broadcast to provide analysis. When he wasn’t appearing on NBC’s regular broadcast, Kornacki could really nerd out. He’d explore far-flung counties, compare their results with Kamala Harris’ and Donald Trump’s numbers from 2024 and explain the significance of even the smallest shifts. It was a fascinating window into how he navigates election nights and just how much information he has rattling around in his head. One moment, he’d be casually exploring Susan Collins’ numbers in Portland, Maine, while chatting with production staff. Sometimes he’d fumble with the board, blow his nose, troubleshoot errors with a producer or work through a distraction as studio chatter filtered into the stream. The next, he’d get a message from a producer in his ear, stand up a little straighter and deliver his analysis to NBC’s broadcast audience in his usual authoritative voice. The unpolished moments only added to the charm. The whole thing was bewildering in the best possible way — total catnip for data nerds and a marvel for anyone with an eye for production. I watched for hours and never saw the viewer count drop below 300,000 on YouTube. NBC has been expanding its reach beyond traditional broadcasts, often putting Kornacki at the forefront. He even has a new paid newsletter called Data Dive with Steve Kornacki . Judging by Tuesday night, I’d say the strategy is paying off. First big call of the night NewsNation’s election night set on Tuesday. (Courtesy of NewsNation) At 9:11 p.m. Eastern time, NewsNation was the first to make the call that Democrats would take control of the House of Representatives. At 9:59 p.m., MS NOW projected the same. Then at 10:04 p.m., CNN made its call. As CNN’s Jake Tapper told viewers, that would make New York Rep. Hakeem Jeffries the first Black speaker of the House. “I have to say, ‘Wow,’” Tapper said. “It’s a bluenami.” NBC’s Steve Kornacki said, “Absolute carnage for Republicans … Democrats are getting everything that they asked for.” CNN’s Dana Bash said, “This is the beginning of Donald Trump’s lame-duck lame duck era.” CNN’s Kaitlan Collins said, “They’re having a watch party at the White House right now, but life has fundamentally changed for everyone inside the White House because of the Democrats regaining control of the House — because they know it means subpoenas and investigations and oversight.” Collins added, “It’s going to be a very different Washington after tonight.” MS NOW’s Rachel Maddow said, “This is a seismic change in Washington.” And MS NOW’s Lawrence O’Donnell said, “This means the Trump reign of terror is over.” Ass-whooping As CNN’s Van Jones celebrated the Democrats’ big night, conservative commentator Scott Jennings, sitting next to him, acknowledged just how disappointing the night was for Republicans. “Well, look, I’m not going to sugarcoat it,” Jennings said. “There’s a political science term for what’s happening. It’s called a country ass-whooping.” Jennings chalked it up to the “cost of living and gas prices.” Trump’s Tarlov tantrums President Donald Trump was watching Fox News on Tuesday afternoon when he announced on Truth Social that he was changing channels because of “Fox & Friends” liberal panelist Jessica Tarlov. Trump wrote, “Watching the Pre-Election Results on FoxNews, but I can’t stand Jessica Tarlov’s voice — DUMB AS A ROCK, but it’s the voice that gets me. Have to go somewhere else until that LOSER is off the air — I’ll be back later!” Trump has long complained about Tarlov, even speaking negatively about her at campaign rallies. At one point on Tuesday, Trump texted “Fox & Friends” co-host Greg Gutfeld, who showed the text on the air. It read: “Tell Tarlov – who is the WORST – gas prices are lower than they were for Biden and Obama, and they allowed Iran to have the nuclear bomb.” Tarlov had a good line, telling Gutfeld to treat Trump’s texts like spam. She said, “Just press STOP.” Meanwhile, Tarlov wasn’t the only thing about Fox News on Tuesday that bothered Trump. He was also upset by a Fox News poll about the midterms. Trump wrote on Truth Social , “The FoxNews Poll, always the worst, is so insulting with the questions they ask. Every question is a hit job, and every answer is ‘BULL(EXPLETIVE!)’ This has been going on for years.” (Of course, the president of the United States didn’t write “EXPLETIVE.”) More Trump Trump was quiet most of election night until finally taking to Truth Social at 10:37 p.m. when it was clear that the GOP was having a bad night. Trump wrote, “I wasn’t on the Ballot and if I was, we would have won IN A LANDSLIDE! Thank you for your attention to this matter. President DONALD J. TRUMP.” Wait, didn’t Trump tell voters to act as if he was on the ballot? Trump says he will accept results The CBS News set during Tuesday night’s election coverage. (Courtesy of CBS News) The New York Times reached Trump before 11 p.m. Eastern and asked if he’d accept the midterm results. Trump told the Times’ Erica L. Green and Tyler Pager , “I am absolutely willing to accept those results. … We’ll work with the Democrats, and we’ll, you know, look forward to working with them. We’ll probably work something out.” He defended what many consider his most unpopular policies and said he did not regret going to war with Iran. Trump said, “They don’t mind Iran, but they probably weren’t happy with the oil prices.​” But Trump told the Wall Street Journal’s Josh Dawsey , “Iran was a piece of it.” Not that he regrets the war. He told Dawsey, “I did the right thing. I would have done it again.” Earlier in the evening, before the polls had closed, Trump was asked by CBS News about the prospect of Republican control. He said, “I have the ultimate power — it’s called veto.” Then, in yet another interview later in the night with CBS News, Trump said, “My message is, congratulations to the Democrats, and I look forward to working with you for the good of our country.” He added, “I feel that I wasn’t on the ballot, and there was nothing that could be done about that. If I was, we would have won.” In a late-night interview on Fox News, Trump reiterated that he would accept the night’s results. King’s comments CNN’s John King had some revealing observations about the Ohio governor’s race, in which Democrat Amy Acton defeated Republican Vivek Ramaswamy. That race was marked by a controversial Ramaswamy TV ad featuring Acton’s mother calling her daughter a liar. But, as it turned out, Acton was removed from her mother’s home when she was 12 and Acton’s stepfather — her mother’s husband — was charged with raping her. King, who had traveled extensively around the country leading up to the election, talked about his time in Ohio. “When I was out there just last week,” King said, “I cannot tell you — conservatives were disgusted (by the commercial). They said, ‘We’re common-sense conservatives; this does not represent our values.” King said a combat veteran told him he would never vote for Ramaswamy because of the ad. And a conservative woman who supported Trump told him, “If he ever brought my childhood into an ad like that, I would snap his neck.” Speaking of ‘Fox & Friends’ Fox News host Jesse Watters seemed pretty sure that it was going to be a rough night for Republicans. Appearing on “Fox & Friends” well before polls closed on the East Coast, he said: “I don’t feel great. I feel like I’m about to play a game that I know I’m gonna lose. I’m just trying not to get hurt. And getting hurt would mean you lose the Senate. And right now the Senate is very much in play! And it’s making me angry! But I knew this was coming. Everybody knew this was coming. It’s just, how bad is it going to be?” An interesting concession As Democrats racked up victories Tuesday night, Fox News anchor Bret Baier asked Sean Hannity if he thought the night would have gone differently if the U.S. had never gone to war with Iran. Hannity said, “I think 100%. I don’t think there’s any doubt about it.” Touching moment MS NOW’s Symone Sanders Townsend reacts to Keisha Lance Bottoms’ victory in Georgia’s governor’s race Tuesday night. (Courtesy of MS NOW) In one of the more touching moments of election night, MS NOW’s Symone Sanders Townsend became emotional while reflecting on the significance of Keisha Lance Bottoms’ victory in Georgia’s governor’s race. Sanders Townsend said, “Today, America has elected its first Black woman as governor in this country. Keisha Lance Bottoms made history tonight, but she didn’t run on making history. She ran on the issues, and I think that resonated with folks in the state. … Whatever else happens tonight, this is a reminder that in America the people have the power. And Georgia has a governor named Keisha.” She added, “I’m over here about to cry, Lord. After the 2024 presidential election, I myself, but Black women all over this country — it felt like a repudiation of us. The fact that in North Carolina, for example, in 2024, every Democrat that was on the ballot statewide won, except Kamala Harris. I’m like, well, what is this? So there were Black women, myself included, that felt like, well, maybe America just does not want us. And Keisha Lance Bottoms went out there, and to be clear, when she first was on the campaign trail, she went out there in some box braids this summer. And I was like, look at the mayor in her box braids. I don’t know if this is gonna help us win it. And she has won. She won going out there, being herself, talking about the issue, connecting with the people, and, you know, for the 92% out there that was feeling like, ‘Oh, maybe this, maybe the country just doesn’t want us.’ Georgia wanted Keisha.” Florida, Florida, Florida Florida was one of the more interesting stories Tuesday night, even though the results largely reflected the state’s political status quo. Byron Donalds became the state’s first Black governor, but he was endorsed by current Gov. Ron DeSantis and is expected to continue governing the way DeSantis has. In addition, incumbent Republican Sen. Ashley Moody, who was appointed to replace Marco Rubio, narrowly defeated Angie Nixon. But both races were closer than expected. CNN’s John King cautioned against reading too much into Democrats’ relatively strong showings, saying, “There are no participation trophies tonight. At the end of the night, you need to win the race. … When it comes to winning power, there are no participation trophies.” Still, after years of Florida voting reliably red in Senate, gubernatorial and presidential elections, there are at least some signs that things might be shifting, and it’s a state that could be in play in 2028. It led King to say, “Welcome back, Florida.” The editorials The editorial boards of The New York Times, The Washington Post and The Wall Street Journal all weighed in on Tuesday’s election results. The New York Times editorial board wrote: “The American People Have Repudiated Trump.” The board wrote, “President Trump has spent the past two years trying to subvert American democracy. On Tuesday, voters stood up to him.” It added, “The outcome creates a crucial check on Mr. Trump’s autocratic behavior. It empowers Democrats to investigate his corruption and law breaking and pass legislation that offers an alternative to his counterproductive agenda. The country already feels changed, and it will in fact be changed in January.” The Washington Post editorial board wrote, “How Trump and Democrats can navigate divided government.” That navigation might not be so easy. The Wall Street Journal editorial board wrote, “The Trump Reckoning Arrives.” It wrote, “Republicans may console themselves that midterm defeats in second presidential terms are common. But this was more than a normal defeat. The loss of Senate seats and governorships in states that had become GOP redoubts will have ramifications for years to come.” Alternatives One of the more interesting election-night livestreams I checked in on was “Election Night 2026 Livestream!” It featured “Chuck Toddcast” host Chuck Todd, former CNN journalist Chris Cillizza, The 19th politics reporter Grace Panetta and Geoffrey Skelley, chief elections analyst for Decision Desk HQ. “The Jim Acosta Show” featured the former CNN anchor alongside Katie Couric and a team of election analysts. Both were good alternatives to traditional cable and broadcast network coverage, offering something different throughout the evening. Cillizza summed up the night after a string of races were called for Democrats: “I think it has to be considered a tsunami.” The final word Former New Jersey Republican Gov. Chris Christie offered this assessment on ABC News: “We’re looking at maybe 240 seats in the House. The Democrats appear to be headed to a majority in the Senate, and I think you’re going to wind up with near-27 Democratic governors. The Trump era is over, and when Greg Abbott, who was extraordinarily popular as a three-term governor, loses tonight in a state like Texas, I don’t care what the president says on Truth Social. The Trump era is over.” Other media news, tidbits and interesting links “60 Minutes” had its best ratings of the season this past Sunday. CBS announced that, according to Nielsen Big Data + Panel live plus same day ratings, the show averaged 10.41 million viewers. As I keep writing about, the show’s ratings are often helped or hurt by whether CBS has the featured late Sunday afternoon NFL game. This past Sunday, CBS did have the late game, with a big showdown between the Denver Broncos and Kansas City Chiefs, which ended in dramatic fashion with Denver holding on to a 28-24 victory. The NBA didn’t schedule any games on Tuesday because of Election Day. It was the fourth time in five years that the league had no games on Election Day. The lone exception was 2025, when there were no major races involving cities where the NBA has teams. The Athletic’s Devon Henderson has more on the NBA and why it doesn’t typically schedule games on Election Day. Mediaite’s Ahmad Austin Jr. with “Chris Cuomo Says He’s Weighing Leaving News Business Due to Violent Urges: ‘Desire to Punch People … Is Consuming Me.’” Awful Announcing’s Brandon Contes with “Joe Scarborough torches Pat McAfee for ruining ‘College GameDay.’” More resources for journalists Invest in your career with Poynter Membership for just $75 a year. Get access to eight essential courses on writing, editing and ethics , exclusive webinars with Poynter experts, practical research tools and early access to Poynter events. Learn how to create vertical video for journalism with Poynter’s new self-directed course. Build pract",
